now when it comes to covert 19 vaccines  south africa has an adequate supply but  it's proving tough to get jabs into  people's arms some are put off by the  long journey to vaccination centers and  others are worried about the side  effects so far just under 10 million  people have been fully vaccinated  against the virus that's less than 25  percent of adults in the country  in the hope of reaching those in rural  areas one organization has set up mobile  vaccination clinics dw's adrian kriesch  has more  it's payday for social grants in the  town of tula maharashi hundreds of  people have traveled in from surrounding  villages the province of pumalanga is  one of the poorest in south africa  and also has one of the country's lowest  vaccination rates that's why health  workers have set up a mobile vaccination  site right next to the queue  it is easier for me when i can get  vaccinated at the same place where i  pick up my social grant they only came  once to my village to vaccinate and i  only learned about it when it was too  late that's why i'm happy i can get it  here today  mobile vaccination sites are being set  up next to welfare offices and  supermarkets where grants are paid out  all over the country the idea take the  vaccine to the people  so in the course of today about 700  people over 60 will collect their grants  we know that people over 60 are far more  vulnerable and far more exposed to  covert and severe consequences from  covet so it was an obvious thing to come  to the queue where older people are  collecting their grants to offer  vaccines because they are already in  town at a place where they are easy to  find and approach and offer the vaccine  less than one in five people in  impumalanga have been vaccinated so far  many people here haven't been vaccinated  because they live in rural areas it is  expensive and difficult for them to  reach vaccination centers  it's not just access to vaccination  that's stopping people getting the jab  there are other worrying reasons says  elizabeth eduard she's been researching  why people in cape town are reluctant to  get vaccinated people's religious  beliefs are one issue she has ideas on  how to combat this  if religion has been pointed out in a  particular  population  then  what would be recommended is what i'll  call or what is generally called the  stakeholders engagement engage religious  leaders opinion leaders community  leaders and  for social media  that's a tough not to crack  those waiting for their social grants in  pumalanga have also heard many rumors  about vaccination side effects but  carlos marwanga isn't worried he says  all his friends who got it are doing  fine  well for more on this i'm joined by  ashraf khajii he's a professor in the  department of psychology at stellenbosch  university thank you very much indeed  for being with us here on dw news africa  now despite the covert 19 vaccine being  available to all south african adults  why is the uptake so low  there are a range of reasons we should  keep in mind that the task of  vaccinating the entire adult population  in a very short space of time is quite  unprecedented usually government  vaccination drives have been targeted at  infants and children and travelers and  there's never been a need to vaccinate  adults on such a large scale because  there's never been a pandemic of this  nature in living memory  the the second thing that i want to say  is that usually people believe that  contact with the health system  is necessary only when they become ill  and so it does require some kind of  psychological shift to accept that a  well person who requires a vaccination  to remain well those are a couple of uh  uh initial reasons but i think also  whenever a new concept or technology is  introduced into a society not everybody  is going to rush at once they'll be  innovators there'll be early adopters  there'll be an early majority and what  we now need is the late majority to step  up and make themselves available for  vaccination okay so could you talk us  through some of the social norms and  behavioral elements that play a part in  this vaccine hesitancy that we're seeing  yeah so the research on social norms  tells us that people's behavior  is often influenced by their perceptions  of what is normal or typical in other  people  so if people understand and believe that  most other people have accepted a  vaccine then there's a strong likelihood  that many other people will follow but  of course this is uh required in the in  the context of information that vaccines  actually save lives  so how do you convince people to take  the jab when there are challenges around  convincing them to do so we need to  counter vaccine  hesitancy we need correct information  based on the latest research we need  easy access to vaccination sites so  vaccination sites should be where people  are taxi ranks train stations  where where farms are uh close to  places where people actually are to be  found in large numbers we need visible  social marketing campaigns and community  champions who encourage acceptance of of  vaccination  so  for example the congress of traditional  leaders of south africa has called on  men to take the covet 19 vaccine because  relatively lower  numbers of men have been  accepting the vaccine compared to women  also religious leaders who have a huge  amount of influence among their  congregation they should continue to be  role models in accepting a vaccine  and then other prominent community  members celebrities sports men and women  health workers elders to  publicly accept a vaccination and  encourage others to do the same all  right  ashraf khajii thank you very much indeed  for your insights
 

